/**
@mainpage HomeCore API

HomeCore is single-threaded C11 bare-metal firmware. It provides a serial shell,
a VFS with RAM files and mounted FAT and littlefs volumes, user sessions, a
1 kHz uptime clock, and the G2BASIC interpreter. This reference covers the public headers under `include/homecore`.
The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core).

@section mainpage_guides Guides

| Guide | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| [Architecture](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/architecture.md) | Layers, startup, memory, and runtime contracts |
| [Development](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/development.md) | Setup, builds, configuration, tests, and debugging |
| [C coding standard](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/coding-standard.md) | Language, style, memory, and interrupt rules |
| @ref storage "Files and storage" | The VFS, mounted filesystems (FAT, littlefs), and block devices |
| [Shell and system services](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/shell.md) | Commands, sessions, files, and uptime |
| [Extending](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/extending.md) | Adding shell commands and VFS device nodes |
| [Porting](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/porting.md) | Adding boards, SoCs, and architecture support |
| [Versioning](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/versioning.md) | SemVer rules, the public interface, and the changelog |

@section mainpage_boards Boards

| Board | Core | Status |
| --- | --- | --- |
| @ref board_lm3s6965evb "LM3S6965EVB" | Cortex-M3, 64 KB RAM | QEMU tested in CI |
| @ref board_stm32f4discovery "STM32F4DISCOVERY" | Cortex-M4F, 128 KB RAM | Builds in CI; hardware validation pending |
| @ref board_stm32vldiscovery "STM32VLDISCOVERY" | Cortex-M3, 8 KB RAM | QEMU tested in CI; hardware validation pending |

@ref boards compares features, memory, and build presets.

@section mainpage_layers Layers

The API is grouped by layer. Each header defines one module group.

| Layer | Modules | Header location |
| --- | --- | --- |
| @ref hal | @ref arch, @ref soc, @ref board | `homecore/arch`, `homecore/soc`, `homecore/board` |
| @ref drivers | @ref console, @ref gpio, @ref led, @ref block | `homecore/drivers` |
| @ref kernel_services | @ref kernel, @ref user, @ref session | `homecore/kernel`, `homecore/user`, `homecore/session` |
| @ref subsystems | @ref vfs, @ref filesystems (@ref fat, @ref littlefs), @ref shell | `homecore/vfs`, `homecore/fs`, `homecore/shell` |

The group tree is on the **Topics** page. Each group page shows its place in the hierarchy. Each header page
shows include graphs in both directions. Structures that reference other
documented types show a collaboration graph.

@section mainpage_libraries Bundled libraries

CMSIS is used only inside the architecture and SoC code; the public API does
not expose its types. Its reference is Arm's documentation for the pinned
release.

| Library | Reference | Used by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| CMSIS 6.3.0 Core (`external/cmsis`) | [CMSIS-Core (Cortex-M)](https://arm-software.github.io/CMSIS_6/v6.3.0/Core/index.html) | NVIC, SysTick, and SCB access in `src/arch`, device headers in `src/soc` |
| STM32F4 device headers (`external/stm32f4`) | Vendored ST CMSIS device headers; no separate reference | The STM32F407 SoC port |
| FatFs R0.16 (`external/fatfs`) | [FatFs documentation](https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/); HomeCore's use is in @ref fat | FAT volumes, compiled only for boards that mount one |
| littlefs v2.11.3 (`external/littlefs`) | [README](https://github.com/littlefs-project/littlefs/blob/v2.11.3/README.md), [design](https://github.com/littlefs-project/littlefs/blob/v2.11.3/DESIGN.md); HomeCore's use is in @ref littlefs | littlefs volumes, compiled only for boards that mount one |

@if g2basic
G2Basic is built into this site from the pinned submodule commit, so it matches
this firmware. Its groups are also listed on the **Topics** page.

| Library | Reference | Used by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| G2Basic (`external/g2basic`) | <a href="g2basic/index.html">G2Basic API</a>, starting with the @ref api | The `basic` shell command, which registers `millis()`, `led()`, and `ledget()` |
@endif

@section mainpage_startup Startup order

`main()` calls arch_init(), soc_init(), board_init(), `dt_init()` (generated
driver initialization), k_init(), `dt_mount_all()` (the board's filesystem
mounts), and shell_init() in that order, then prints the banner and enters
shell_run(). A module may
depend only on modules initialized before it. For example, soc_init() registers
VFS devices but must not print, because the console is not open until k_init().

@section mainpage_scope Scope

The firmware has no scheduler, privilege separation, authentication, or
persistent storage: every mounted volume is a ramdisk formatted at boot. The architecture thread primitives are scaffolding; the
SVC and PendSV handlers they depend on currently panic.
*/

/**
@defgroup hal Hardware abstraction
@brief CPU, chip, and board layers under the portable code.

Portable kernel and subsystem code uses these interfaces instead of register
addresses or pin assignments. Each target selects one implementation of each
interface at configure time through `HOMECORE_BOARD`.
*/

/**
@defgroup drivers Drivers
@brief Device drivers instantiated from the compile-time device description.

`soc.yaml` and `board.yaml` describe the hardware; the generator creates each
enabled device's configuration and calls its driver from `dt_init()`, after
the devices it references. Driver headers are internal to `src/drivers/`; the
public parts are the interfaces other code uses: the console, GPIO ports,
LEDs, and block devices. See the Development guide's "Device description" section.
*/

/**
@defgroup kernel_services Kernel services
@brief Kernel initialization, time, heap statistics, users, and sessions.
*/

/**
@defgroup subsystems Subsystems
@brief Portable services built on the kernel: the VFS, filesystems, and the command shell.
*/

/**
@defgroup filesystems Filesystems
@ingroup subsystems
@brief Volumes on block devices, mounted into the VFS.

A filesystem implements ::vfs_fs_ops_t and owns every path below its mount
point. Boards list their mounts in the device description, and the generated
`dt_mount_all()` mounts them at startup; each library is compiled only when a
board uses it.

| Filesystem | Suited to | Mount function |
| --- | --- | --- |
| @ref fat | Removable media shared with other systems | fs_fat_mount() |
| @ref littlefs | Storage the firmware owns; survives resets during writes | fs_littlefs_mount() |

@ref storage "Files and storage" compares them and describes mounting, path
resolution, and descriptors across the whole VFS.
*/
